1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:02,400
Just in case you haven't had

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,335
enough fun with
kernel locking yet,

3
00:00:04,335 --> 00:00:07,185
we're going to have more fun
with locking in this video.

4
00:00:07,185 --> 00:00:08,910
This is really just
a continuation of

5
00:00:08,910 --> 00:00:11,670
the previous series that
we've got about locking.

6
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:13,590
We're going to
introduce spinlocks

7
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:14,700
and we're going to talk more

8
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:19,015
about locking strategies and
dealing with lock ordering.

9
00:00:19,015 --> 00:00:23,075
Spinlocks are an
important type of

10
00:00:23,075 --> 00:00:25,490
locking mechanism that can

11
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,935
be used in code
that cannot sleep.

12
00:00:27,935 --> 00:00:29,540
An example of code that can't

13
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:31,490
sleep is interrupt handlers.

14
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:35,505
If you need to do some
locking in interrupt handler,

15
00:00:35,505 --> 00:00:38,630
then the locking option

16
00:00:38,630 --> 00:00:40,955
that you have is really
spinlocks and nothing else.

17
00:00:40,955 --> 00:00:42,620
It's not safe for you to sleep

18
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:44,785
within interrupt
handler contexts.

19
00:00:44,785 --> 00:00:46,815
They're very important
for that reason.

20
00:00:46,815 --> 00:00:49,070
They're also very useful in

21
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:51,260
cases where there's
something short and

22
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:53,540
simple that you need
to do because they're

23
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:55,850
higher performance
than semaphores,

24
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:57,905
and therefore probably
a better choice for

25
00:00:57,905 --> 00:01:01,125
that type of situation.

26
00:01:01,125 --> 00:01:03,845
Conceptually, what
a spinlock is,

27
00:01:03,845 --> 00:01:05,270
is just a single bit in

28
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:08,030
integer value plus a
type loop spin that

29
00:01:08,030 --> 00:01:13,610
happens waiting for that
bit to get set or cleared.

30
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:16,670
There's an atomic test and

31
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:19,760
set of the bit that can
happen all in one operation.

32
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:22,175
In other words,
there's essentially

33
00:01:22,175 --> 00:01:24,590
a single cycle
operation that says,

34
00:01:24,590 --> 00:01:27,865
"If the bits not set yet,
I'm going to set it."

35
00:01:27,865 --> 00:01:31,650
That way if two
different threads

36
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:33,395
were attempting to do
that at the same time,

37
00:01:33,395 --> 00:01:35,840
only one of those two threads
would actually be able

38
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:38,390
to see that the bit
wasn't set and set it.

39
00:01:38,390 --> 00:01:39,560
The other one would continue

40
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,210
waiting to be able
to set the bit.

41
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:45,770
An important thing to know
about spinlocks though,

42
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:47,900
is that any of the
processes that are

43
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,240
waiting on access to a spinlock,

44
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:51,620
are executing in a tight loop

45
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:53,090
just calling that
over and over again,

46
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:56,745
waiting to see if the
bit is still set or not.

47
00:01:56,745 --> 00:01:59,015
This is another reason why

48
00:01:59,015 --> 00:02:01,660
there's the caveat here
about being properly used,

49
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:03,380
because if you hold

50
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:05,705
a spinlock for a very
large amount of time,

51
00:02:05,705 --> 00:02:08,000
what it means is that
any other CPU that's

52
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,280
waiting is just
burning CPU cycles,

53
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:12,260
pulling spinlock
over and over again.

54
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:14,045
In which case it's probably not

55
00:02:14,045 --> 00:02:17,550
a very good performance
solution at all.

56
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:21,590
The other thing that
we have with spinlocks

57
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:25,000
are irqsave and
irqrestore variance.

58
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,230
These might sound similar to

59
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:29,000
the interruptible things that

60
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,655
we've talked about
with semaphores,

61
00:02:30,655 --> 00:02:32,660
but they're actually
completely different so

62
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:35,710
you shouldn't get
these confused.

63
00:02:35,710 --> 00:02:39,180
The irqsave and
irqrestore versions;

64
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:40,650
remember we talked
about the fact

65
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:42,890
that spinlocks are
really your option

66
00:02:42,890 --> 00:02:47,160
for doing locking in
kernel interrupts.

67
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:50,300
If you're going to have
a lock that you use

68
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,025
both inside kernel interrupts

69
00:02:52,025 --> 00:02:53,840
and outside kernel interrupts,

70
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:55,430
it's important that you use

71
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:59,620
the irqsave versions for
accessing those spinlocks.

72
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:03,695
The irqsave and
irqrestore versions

73
00:03:03,695 --> 00:03:05,240
are always safe in either

74
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:06,970
interrupt or non
interrupt contexts.

75
00:03:06,970 --> 00:03:09,470
If I'm using a locking
either context,

76
00:03:09,470 --> 00:03:13,025
it's always safe for me to
use the irqsave version.

77
00:03:13,025 --> 00:03:16,730
The difference between
these two versions is

78
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:18,260
the irqsave version
is going to have

79
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:20,525
an extra argument
here with flags,

80
00:03:20,525 --> 00:03:21,950
and the flags can just be

81
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:25,010
a local value that you
store on the stack.

82
00:03:25,010 --> 00:03:27,500
Here I'm showing a simple
example where I've

83
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,365
got a value that I'm incrementing
in a data structure,

84
00:03:30,365 --> 00:03:32,180
and I've got a lock
that's a spinlock

85
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:33,950
that's associated with
that data structure.

86
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:37,165
I've initialized that already
here with spinlock in it.

87
00:03:37,165 --> 00:03:40,175
The irqsave, what
this is going to do

88
00:03:40,175 --> 00:03:43,475
is check to see if
interrupts are enabled,

89
00:03:43,475 --> 00:03:45,920
if they are enabled, it's
going to disable interrupts,

90
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:47,570
and it's going to
set up the flag to

91
00:03:47,570 --> 00:03:49,475
track whether interrupts
we're enabled.

92
00:03:49,475 --> 00:03:53,900
So then I can access my
variable and then I can use

93
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:56,330
irqrestore to look at
the flags value and

94
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:59,540
decide if interrupts
were initially enabled,

95
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:01,460
then it will re-enable

96
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:05,160
them at the completion
of the irqrestore call.

97
00:04:06,250 --> 00:04:10,985
The difference between these
two is really this check for

98
00:04:10,985 --> 00:04:15,500
interrupts enabled and
disable re-enable step.

99
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:18,020
If I use the base
spinlock versions

100
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:20,780
that don't have the
irqsave and irqrestore,

101
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:22,370
I'm not passing that flag value

102
00:04:22,370 --> 00:04:24,425
and I'm never going to
be disabling interrupts.

103
00:04:24,425 --> 00:04:26,510
Interrupts are free
to happen even

104
00:04:26,510 --> 00:04:30,310
though I have obtained
the spinlock.

105
00:04:32,390 --> 00:04:34,985
The spinlock version again,

106
00:04:34,985 --> 00:04:37,880
the generic one without
irqsave and restore,

107
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:40,280
it doesn't disable or
re-enable interrupts.

108
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:42,320
It's only safe to use this if

109
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:46,715
you are never using the
spinlock in an interrupt,

110
00:04:46,715 --> 00:04:49,280
or if you happen to know
that interrupts are blocked

111
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:52,390
for some other reason when
this code is executing.

112
00:04:52,390 --> 00:04:54,680
If you think about
what could happen

113
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:56,150
if an interrupt happens on

114
00:04:56,150 --> 00:04:58,550
the same CPU and it

115
00:04:58,550 --> 00:05:01,945
attempts to access data lock
that's held with spinlock-

116
00:05:01,945 --> 00:05:03,450
In that case, you're
going to deadlock

117
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:05,790
because the code is

118
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:07,395
just going to spin
in a tight loop

119
00:05:07,395 --> 00:05:09,705
attempting to access
the spinlock,

120
00:05:09,705 --> 00:05:10,860
but the spinlock's already

121
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:12,810
accessed with a thread
that stalled on

122
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:15,210
the same CPU and so
you're never going to

123
00:05:15,210 --> 00:05:18,005
be able to continue
in that case.

124
00:05:18,005 --> 00:05:19,650
You have to be careful about

125
00:05:19,650 --> 00:05:21,000
the way you're using spinlocks

126
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,840
to know if you're not
going to use the IRQ;

127
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:26,415
one, you have to be sure that

128
00:05:26,415 --> 00:05:29,500
your code's never going to
be called from an interrupt.

129
00:05:31,010 --> 00:05:36,040
In terms of using spinlocks,

130
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:38,375
there are some restrictions
then that we have in terms

131
00:05:38,375 --> 00:05:41,685
of what we can do while
we're holding a spinlock.

132
00:05:41,685 --> 00:05:45,480
Again, because spinlocks are
going to be used in cases

133
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:50,590
like interrupts where you
aren't able to sleep.

134
00:05:52,910 --> 00:05:56,945
The code rule is really
just that any code

135
00:05:56,945 --> 00:05:59,550
that is holding a spinlock

136
00:05:59,550 --> 00:06:03,040
needs to be what we're
going to call atomic.

137
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:07,305
When we talk about
atomic in this context,

138
00:06:07,305 --> 00:06:08,730
is maybe a little
different than when

139
00:06:08,730 --> 00:06:10,200
we're talking about
atomic access to

140
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:11,790
variables so we're creating

141
00:06:11,790 --> 00:06:13,875
an atomic section with
a critical section.

142
00:06:13,875 --> 00:06:17,700
But when we talk about atomic
context for spinlocks,

143
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:22,710
what we really mean is that
the code cannot sleep,

144
00:06:22,710 --> 00:06:24,180
and it also can't relinquish

145
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:26,640
the processor for anything
other than interrupts

146
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:28,240
and the only way that it can

147
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:29,925
relinquish the processor
for interrupts

148
00:06:29,925 --> 00:06:31,515
is if you know that there's

149
00:06:31,515 --> 00:06:34,260
no interrupt that's going
to access your lock.

150
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:37,245
You might ask, "Well,
how would I know?

151
00:06:37,245 --> 00:06:40,215
I know that I can't call sleep,

152
00:06:40,215 --> 00:06:42,750
if I'm calling another
kernel function,

153
00:06:42,750 --> 00:06:43,980
how would I know if that

154
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:46,230
kernel function
doesn't call sleep?"

155
00:06:46,230 --> 00:06:50,835
That's the biggest issue
with using spinlock.

156
00:06:50,835 --> 00:06:54,245
It's difficult to know
that because you really

157
00:06:54,245 --> 00:06:55,590
need to know a lot

158
00:06:55,590 --> 00:06:57,705
about the function
that you're calling.

159
00:06:57,705 --> 00:07:00,245
It could be that the function
you call doesn't sleep,

160
00:07:00,245 --> 00:07:03,300
but maybe that function calls
another function that under

161
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:05,190
some specific scenarios might

162
00:07:05,190 --> 00:07:06,960
came out like a block of memory.

163
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,030
In that case, eventually,

164
00:07:09,030 --> 00:07:12,160
you could get in this
situation where it's sleeping.

165
00:07:12,950 --> 00:07:15,600
Most functions that might

166
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:18,400
allocate memory
also might sleep.

167
00:07:19,550 --> 00:07:22,530
If you're looking at
doing something even

168
00:07:22,530 --> 00:07:24,570
remotely complex to
where it's difficult

169
00:07:24,570 --> 00:07:26,380
to map what kernel functions

170
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:27,780
you're going to be calling or

171
00:07:27,780 --> 00:07:30,435
the state of those functions

172
00:07:30,435 --> 00:07:33,095
with respect to sleep is
not clearly documented,

173
00:07:33,095 --> 00:07:34,800
you're probably
not in a position

174
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:37,245
that you should be
using spinlocks.

175
00:07:37,245 --> 00:07:39,900
The second rule here
is that it must

176
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:41,850
be held for a minimum time

177
00:07:41,850 --> 00:07:43,745
possible because anyone
waiting for the lock

178
00:07:43,745 --> 00:07:45,830
is spinning in tight
CPU wait loops.

179
00:07:45,830 --> 00:07:49,065
Again, if you have a
lot of work to do,

180
00:07:49,065 --> 00:07:50,740
you're calling a bunch
of kernel functions or

181
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:52,420
you're calling anything
that might delay,

182
00:07:52,420 --> 00:07:54,030
for instance,
waiting on hardware,

183
00:07:54,030 --> 00:07:56,480
spinlock is not going to be
the right primitive to use.

184
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:00,280
You should be using semaphores
or something like it.

185
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:06,540
The book talks a lot about
defining locking rules as

186
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:12,195
an important design part of
designing your kernel driver.

187
00:08:12,195 --> 00:08:14,520
Locking rules need
to be explicit

188
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:16,575
and they need to be defined
from the beginning.

189
00:08:16,575 --> 00:08:18,070
You need to plan out for

190
00:08:18,070 --> 00:08:21,510
your driver what needs to
be locked and exactly when

191
00:08:21,510 --> 00:08:24,810
I'm going to be locking
and when I'm going to be

192
00:08:24,810 --> 00:08:30,185
freeing access to whatever
resources that need locking.

193
00:08:30,185 --> 00:08:33,550
The general strategy here
is to define lock that

194
00:08:33,550 --> 00:08:36,420
controls access
to specific data,

195
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:38,115
designing it in
from the beginning.

196
00:08:38,115 --> 00:08:39,630
Don't wait for
locking problems to

197
00:08:39,630 --> 00:08:41,535
happen and then figure
out how to solve them.

198
00:08:41,535 --> 00:08:44,010
Think from the beginning
about what needs locking

199
00:08:44,010 --> 00:08:47,150
and how you can design it in.

200
00:08:47,150 --> 00:08:49,200
Then clearly enforce rules

201
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,335
because you don't want
to end up in a situation

202
00:08:51,335 --> 00:08:53,760
where there's any ambiguity

203
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:56,360
about who needs to hold
and acquire the lock.

204
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:58,965
Because if there is,
you could end up with

205
00:08:58,965 --> 00:09:01,180
nested function call
where two calls in

206
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:03,880
a row attempt to
obtain the same lock,

207
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:07,060
which could end up in
a deadlock scenario.

208
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,585
You always want to write
functions that would assume

209
00:09:12,585 --> 00:09:14,190
that the caller has allocated

210
00:09:14,190 --> 00:09:16,355
the lock and then this
is really important,

211
00:09:16,355 --> 00:09:17,685
when you do assume that,

212
00:09:17,685 --> 00:09:20,410
you want to document that
explicitly in your function.

213
00:09:20,410 --> 00:09:22,520
Don't make your
users read through

214
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:25,300
every single function to know
what your assumptions were.

215
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:28,060
Make sure you're documenting
that with your code.

216
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:33,690
The book talks a little
bit about how to handle

217
00:09:33,690 --> 00:09:36,610
multiple locks and
the sequencing

218
00:09:36,610 --> 00:09:38,445
and ordering for multiple locks.

219
00:09:38,445 --> 00:09:40,770
You can think of a
case where you've got

220
00:09:40,770 --> 00:09:42,185
multiple locks and you need to

221
00:09:42,185 --> 00:09:44,535
acquire them from two
different threads.

222
00:09:44,535 --> 00:09:47,855
Thinking of a case
where we happen

223
00:09:47,855 --> 00:09:51,060
to catch the system
at a snapshot in time

224
00:09:51,060 --> 00:09:55,409
as shown here where we've got
one thread that holds lock1

225
00:09:55,409 --> 00:09:59,650
and the instruction pointer

226
00:09:59,650 --> 00:10:01,595
is currently getting ready
to execute this line

227
00:10:01,595 --> 00:10:04,050
of code here that's about
to grab lock number 2.

228
00:10:04,050 --> 00:10:05,880
Then we think of another thread

229
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:08,510
that doesn't hold
any locks yet and

230
00:10:08,510 --> 00:10:09,460
the instruction pointer is

231
00:10:09,460 --> 00:10:10,770
also at the point
where it's just

232
00:10:10,770 --> 00:10:13,630
getting ready to hold lock2.

233
00:10:13,630 --> 00:10:15,490
What would happen in this case

234
00:10:15,490 --> 00:10:17,030
when the thread number 2 here

235
00:10:17,030 --> 00:10:21,590
executes this instruction
right before thread number 1?

236
00:10:21,590 --> 00:10:23,510
What would happen in last case?

237
00:10:23,510 --> 00:10:25,910
We acquire lock number
2 and thread number 2,

238
00:10:25,910 --> 00:10:29,165
we would still hold lock
number 1 and thread number 1,

239
00:10:29,165 --> 00:10:30,680
and each thread would
be waiting for the

240
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:32,450
other to release its lock,

241
00:10:32,450 --> 00:10:34,100
which, of course, isn't
going to happen because

242
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:36,350
they're both waiting
on mutex_lock.

243
00:10:36,350 --> 00:10:38,900
This is an example
of a deadlock.

244
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:40,940
The real-time
embedded course goes

245
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:42,695
into a lot more
detail about this,

246
00:10:42,695 --> 00:10:46,130
we're just bringing it up
because it's relevant to

247
00:10:46,130 --> 00:10:48,050
our discussion about locking and

248
00:10:48,050 --> 00:10:51,600
specifically about multiple
locks and lock ordering.

249
00:10:52,390 --> 00:10:55,130
Some rules we can use for lock

250
00:10:55,130 --> 00:10:57,155
ordering when we're talking
about multiple locks.

251
00:10:57,155 --> 00:10:58,550
Lock should always be acquired

252
00:10:58,550 --> 00:10:59,960
in the same order and then they

253
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,700
should be released in
the opposite order

254
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:03,155
that they were required.

255
00:11:03,155 --> 00:11:06,275
A lot of times the
lock ordering rules,

256
00:11:06,275 --> 00:11:07,850
if you're talking
about how to use

257
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:10,115
locks that are already
used by the kernel,

258
00:11:10,115 --> 00:11:13,475
are not very well-documented.

259
00:11:13,475 --> 00:11:15,140
There's an expectation
that you're

260
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:16,895
going to read the
kernel code and

261
00:11:16,895 --> 00:11:19,040
understand any lock ordering of

262
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:21,965
multiple locks if you need
to obtain multiple locks.

263
00:11:21,965 --> 00:11:23,330
It's really to read the Source

264
00:11:23,330 --> 00:11:24,605
Luke that we've talked about,

265
00:11:24,605 --> 00:11:26,240
in other cases, that

266
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:27,620
the kernel developers
are going to expect

267
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:29,420
you to read the source.

268
00:11:29,420 --> 00:11:31,670
In the case that you're
designing something

269
00:11:31,670 --> 00:11:33,800
that's using multiple
locks you define,

270
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:36,350
then you'll need to explicitly

271
00:11:36,350 --> 00:11:40,025
state that in your
own documentation.

272
00:11:40,025 --> 00:11:42,500
General rules of
thumb about how to do

273
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:44,615
lock ordering and how
to use multiple locks.

274
00:11:44,615 --> 00:11:46,190
Number 1 is if you can

275
00:11:46,190 --> 00:11:47,840
design such that you
don't need to use

276
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:49,580
multiple locks and you
don't ever need to

277
00:11:49,580 --> 00:11:52,190
acquire multiple locks,
that's the best case.

278
00:11:52,190 --> 00:11:53,960
When you get to the
point where you

279
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:55,625
think you need multiple locks,

280
00:11:55,625 --> 00:11:59,015
maybe one step would be to
step back and evaluate;

281
00:11:59,015 --> 00:12:00,080
is that really what I need?

282
00:12:00,080 --> 00:12:01,220
Or is there some way I could do

283
00:12:01,220 --> 00:12:04,265
this that wouldn't
require multiple locks?

284
00:12:04,265 --> 00:12:06,545
The book also
suggests you should

285
00:12:06,545 --> 00:12:08,540
obtain your own
driver locks before

286
00:12:08,540 --> 00:12:11,120
you obtain locks that are
used in other part of

287
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:13,910
the kernel. Why would
you want to do this?

288
00:12:13,910 --> 00:12:16,550
Well, it's because you've
got more control over

289
00:12:16,550 --> 00:12:18,725
your lock than the kernel lock

290
00:12:18,725 --> 00:12:20,840
and there might
be other drivers.

291
00:12:20,840 --> 00:12:23,570
Essentially, the lock that

292
00:12:23,570 --> 00:12:26,030
the kernel defines might
be a more popular lock,

293
00:12:26,030 --> 00:12:27,680
there might be more drivers

294
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:30,005
that are going to try
to use that lock.

295
00:12:30,005 --> 00:12:32,330
You want to minimize
the chance that you're

296
00:12:32,330 --> 00:12:33,920
going to be blocking

297
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:35,690
someone when you're holding

298
00:12:35,690 --> 00:12:38,130
that more popular kernel lock.

299
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:40,670
Always hold semaphores before

300
00:12:40,670 --> 00:12:43,070
spinlocks based on what
we already talked about.

301
00:12:43,070 --> 00:12:44,270
In terms of the
difference between

302
00:12:44,270 --> 00:12:45,290
semaphores and spinlocks,

303
00:12:45,290 --> 00:12:48,140
you can probably guess that
it'd be important to do that.

304
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:50,870
Because if you remember
our rule about

305
00:12:50,870 --> 00:12:52,910
spinlock and the contexts

306
00:12:52,910 --> 00:12:54,500
that you have when you're
holding a spinlock,

307
00:12:54,500 --> 00:12:56,315
it's important not to sleep,

308
00:12:56,315 --> 00:12:57,650
but a semaphore can

309
00:12:57,650 --> 00:12:59,510
sleep when you're
attempting to access it.

310
00:12:59,510 --> 00:13:01,460
Therefore, it's
not safe to try to

311
00:13:01,460 --> 00:13:04,940
acquire a semaphore when
you're holding a spinlock.

312
00:13:05,910 --> 00:13:09,910
The book also wraps up Chapter 5

313
00:13:09,910 --> 00:13:13,275
with some talk about
alternatives to locking.

314
00:13:13,275 --> 00:13:16,520
One of them is atomic
variables and bit operations.

315
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:18,560
If you're just worried
about the case that we

316
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:20,540
talked about at the
very beginning where

317
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:23,240
you're assigning or doing

318
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:26,240
incrementing or simple
operations on variables,

319
00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:29,345
and you just need to know that
that operation is atomic,

320
00:13:29,345 --> 00:13:31,490
there are atomic types that take

321
00:13:31,490 --> 00:13:33,560
care of that for
you so that you can

322
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:36,035
know across platforms that

323
00:13:36,035 --> 00:13:38,360
the access to the variable
is going to be atomic.

324
00:13:38,360 --> 00:13:40,250
That's a way you can
solve problems like that

325
00:13:40,250 --> 00:13:42,785
without having to resort
to locking at all.

326
00:13:42,785 --> 00:13:45,350
There's also bit operations

327
00:13:45,350 --> 00:13:47,900
or similar way that you can do

328
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:49,340
the checks that we talked

329
00:13:49,340 --> 00:13:50,450
about that happen under the hood

330
00:13:50,450 --> 00:13:53,510
for semaphores like
atomic test-and-set.

331
00:13:53,510 --> 00:13:55,970
All that stuff is
provided for you.

332
00:13:55,970 --> 00:13:58,400
This is another way
that you could maybe

333
00:13:58,400 --> 00:13:59,600
accomplish your goals without

334
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:01,640
having to use locking at all.

335
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:03,830
Then there are
also ways that you

336
00:14:03,830 --> 00:14:05,555
can use lock-free algorithms.

337
00:14:05,555 --> 00:14:07,475
The book talks about if you

338
00:14:07,475 --> 00:14:09,710
carefully design a
circular buffer with

339
00:14:09,710 --> 00:14:12,800
atomic count values there

340
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:14,945
can be two different
threads accessing it,

341
00:14:14,945 --> 00:14:16,070
a reader and writer.

342
00:14:16,070 --> 00:14:19,160
It turns out you actually
don't need locks in that case,

343
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:21,440
and the book
describes how you can

344
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:25,055
implement that particular
type of circular buffer.

345
00:14:25,055 --> 00:14:27,050
Then there's also read-copy

346
00:14:27,050 --> 00:14:29,120
update that the
book talks about,

347
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:32,240
which is an interesting
concept where you have

348
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:36,470
old copies of variables
that remain valid.

349
00:14:36,470 --> 00:14:39,215
As a new value is written,

350
00:14:39,215 --> 00:14:43,880
there's new accesses
to the variable.

351
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:45,305
We'll use that new value.

352
00:14:45,305 --> 00:14:47,930
When old accesses are released,

353
00:14:47,930 --> 00:14:50,150
the old variable values will be

354
00:14:50,150 --> 00:14:52,520
cleaned up and the memory
will be deallocated.

355
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:56,885
This is another way that
you could handle access to

356
00:14:56,885 --> 00:14:58,970
or concurrent shared access to

357
00:14:58,970 --> 00:15:02,100
resource without
resorting to locking.